    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
    <h1>Hanoi Scooter Rental: Automatic Scooter Pre Ride Checks Selection Guide</h1>
    
    <p>Every rental counter conversation ends the same way: two or three machines of roughly the right size, and a choice between them. Most renters make that choice with their eyes, picking the newest fairing or the friendliest colour. The better instrument is the pre-ride check itself. Run the walk-around on each candidate before you choose, and the machine reveals its maintenance history, its suitability for your week and its future behaviour in ways no panel gap can hide.</p>
    <p>This selection guide turns the standard pre-ride check into a comparison tool: what to test on each candidate, which results should end the conversation, and how to weigh what you find against price. It sits in the <a href="/hanoi-scooter-rental/">Hanoi scooter rental</a> cluster; rates and deposits are covered in the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> overview and longer stays in the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental Hanoi</a> guide.</p>

    <nav class="toc"><ol><li><a href="#sec-1">Why the walk-around selects the machine</a></li><li><a href="#sec-2">Testing brakes before you choose</a></li><li><a href="#sec-3">Tyres, wheels and the story they tell</a></li><li><a href="#sec-4">Engine, transmission and cold start</a></li><li><a href="#sec-5">Lights, horn and the electronics</a></li><li><a href="#sec-6">Storage, locks and daily equipment</a></li><li><a href="#sec-7">Weighing findings against price</a></li><li><a href="#sec-8">A five-minute counter routine</a></li></ol></nav>

    <section id="sec-1">
      <h2 id="sec-1">Why the walk-around selects the machine</h2>
      <p>A rental scooter at rest tells you most of what matters about its week. Machines that pass a careful walk-around cleanly have been maintained, and machines that fail small tests at the counter will keep failing them all rental long. The check costs five minutes and cannot be done after the papers are signed, which is exactly why it belongs before the choice rather than after it.</p>
      <p>Run the same check on every candidate, in the same order, so that the comparison is fair. Brakes, tyres, engine, lights, storage: a fixed sequence keeps you honest when one machine is newer in one area and worse in another. Renters who check in a different order each time end up choosing whichever machine they checked most recently, which is not a selection method.</p>
      <p>Treat anything the check reveals as information, not as a verdict by itself. A worn tyre on an otherwise perfect machine is a question for the counter; a soft brake on any machine is a reason to look at the next candidate. The section at the end of this guide covers how to weigh what you find, but the collection step is the same every time: test, note, compare.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="sec-2">
      <h2 id="sec-2">Testing brakes before you choose</h2>
      <p>The brake test decides more than any other item, because brakes are the only system whose failure cannot be managed with planning. Squeeze both levers with the machine parked: the bite should be firm and progressive, with the lever stopping well short of the bar. Pump them a few times and feel for sponginess, which suggests air in the line, or a grabby, all-at-once bite, which suggests pads at the end of their life.</p>
      <p>Roll the machine slowly, in whatever space the yard offers, and test both brakes from moving. The front should stop the machine confidently without noise; the rear should be featherable, because rear-brake control is the backbone of low-speed riding in the districts. Listen for the grinding of metal on metal, which is a sound that ends the candidacy of any machine, and for the click of a dragging caliper that says the machine has been parked rather than serviced.</p>
      <p>Compare results between candidates honestly. A brake that feels slightly softer than another is a data point, not a disqualification, if it still bites progressively and without noise. A brake that cannot be described without the words soft, spongy, grinding or delayed is a rejected machine regardless of its price, colour or mileage. Brakes are the one system where the walk-around accepts no negotiation.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="sec-3">
      <h2 id="sec-3">Tyres, wheels and the story they tell</h2>
      <p>Tyres carry the machine story in tread and sidewall. Look for even tread across the whole width: worn edges mean the machine has lived in the districts and been leaned constantly; a flat centre band means long avenue kilometres. Neither is a problem alone, but a tyre at or near the wear bars is a machine asking for maintenance you would rather the operator paid for.</p>
      <p>Check the sidewalls for cracks, cuts and plugs, and look for dry rot on machines that have stood unused for a while, which is common in rental fleets where some models are in seasonal demand. Spin each wheel if you can lift it, or roll the machine and watch for wobbles that say the rim is bent from a kerb or pothole. A wobbling wheel on a rental is not a quirk; it is a future steering problem at exactly the speeds where stability matters.</p>
      <p>Tyre pressure belongs on the check too, because it changes both the braking test and the low-speed behaviour that fills most Hanoi riding. Ask at the counter what pressure the machine should carry and whether it was set this morning. Under-inflated tyres mask themselves as comfort for the first hour and then cost range, control and tyre life for the rest of the week.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="sec-4">
      <h2 id="sec-4">Engine, transmission and cold start</h2>
      <p>Ask to hear the machine start cold, not warm from a previous customer ride. A cold start tells you about the battery, the starter and the fuel system in one move; a machine that has been warmed up for you is a machine whose hardest moment was hidden from you. Once started, listen to the idle: steady, without hunting or racing, and without the rattles of a loose fairing that will accompany you through every alley.</p>
      <p>Roll on the throttle gently and listen to the transmission. An automatic should pick up smoothly, without the revs flaring ahead of the speed, which suggests a belt or clutch at the end of its adjustment. A slipping, whining or groaning transmission is a machine between services, and on a rental it is the operator problem to fix before your week, not during it.</p>
      <p>Look under the machine before and after the engine has run: fresh oil spots, coolant drops or a damp transmission case are all messages about maintenance. For longer rentals, ask directly when the oil was last changed and whether the belt has been inspected. The answers, as much as the sounds, tell you which candidate has been looked after rather than merely rented out. Renters new to automatics can pair this guide with the <a href="/hanoi-scooter-rental/automatic-scooter-beginners-selection-guide/">automatic scooter for beginners selection guide</a> for the riding side of the same decision.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="sec-5">
      <h2 id="sec-5">Lights, horn and the electronics</h2>
      <p>The electronics check is quick and non-negotiable in Hanoi, where the riding day extends into the evening. Test the headlight on both beams, both brake lights front and rear, and every indicator if the machine carries them. A failed brake light is not a minor fault here; it is the signal most following drivers read, and a rental without it asks you to ride in traffic that cannot see you slowing.</p>
      <p>The horn belongs with the lights, because in dense traffic it is an active safety system, not an accessory. Test it with the engine off and running, and prefer the machine whose horn cuts through the yard noise. While the key is in the ignition, confirm the steering lock works, and check that the seat latch and any under-seat lock operate smoothly, because a lock that fights you will do so every time you park.</p>
      <p>Test the instruments too: the fuel gauge or charge display should respond, and the lights should work at the switch. Electronics faults on a rental are small to fix and annoying to live with, and they are the cheapest thing to catch at the counter, where fixing them is the operator task. A machine with a dead gauge and a perfect engine is still a machine you ride blind on exactly the number that matters for your plan.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="sec-6">
      <h2 id="sec-6">Storage, locks and daily equipment</h2>
      <p>The equipment check is where the selection meets your week. Open the seat: does the bay hold your helmet, your lock and the day bag? Try the helmet in rather than estimating, because bay shapes vary between models even at the same size class. A machine that stores your full gear rides differently from day one, and it removes the daily decision about what to carry.</p>
      <p>Ask what comes with the rental: helmet or helmets, a lock, a rain cover, a phone mount. Each item is a conversation at the counter and a daily convenience afterwards. Check the helmet you are offered as carefully as the machine: a sound shell, a working strap and a fit that sits square on your head. The helmet protects you regardless of which candidate scooter wins the comparison.</p>
      <p>Locks deserve their own minute. A disc lock or chain that is included but worn is a false economy; confirm the lock works, the keys are present, and you know where it attaches on the machine. Riders whose routes park in attended patches all day need a lock less than riders parking on streets, and the honest answer about your routes should come before the choice of how much weight to give this item. The two-up version of the same equipment questions is covered in the <a href="/hanoi-scooter-rental/automatic-scooter-with-passenger-selection-guide/">automatic scooter with passenger selection guide</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="sec-7">
      <h2 id="sec-7">Weighing findings against price</h2>
      <p>No candidate passes everything, and price is how the yard acknowledges that. At this operator, compact automatics like the Honda Click and Yamaha Mio rent at 150,000 VND per day and 700,000 VND per week, while larger machines like the Honda Vision and Honda Air Blade rent at 200,000 VND per day and 1,000,000 VND per week. A slightly scuffed panel on the cheaper machine is normal rental life; a soft brake at any price is not.</p>
      <p>Sort findings into three columns before deciding: safety items, comfort items and cosmetic items. Safety findings, brakes, tyres, lights, a slipping transmission, decide the candidacy. Comfort findings, a firmer seat, more storage, a smoother throttle, justify a price step. Cosmetic findings, scratches and scuffs, are worth a photograph for the return conversation and nothing more. Renters who let cosmetics drive the choice pay for the wrong things.</p>
      <p>Then compare the finalists on running cost and fit rather than looks. The differences between models inside the same size class are small in the hand and large over a month, so price the step between classes honestly against your routes. A weekly renter crossing the city daily gets value from the larger class; a district renter gets the same value from the compact one, and the walk-around confirms that the cheaper machine is not the worse machine.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="sec-8">
      <h2 id="sec-8">A five-minute counter routine</h2>
      <p>Turn the whole selection into a fixed five-minute routine. Brakes first: parked squeeze, pump, slow rolling test. Tyres second: tread, sidewalls, pressure question. Engine third: cold start, idle, throttle response, look underneath. Fourth, lights, horn, locks and seat. Fifth, equipment: helmet, lock, storage fit. Same order, every machine, every time.</p>
      <p>Record the results on your phone, three lines per machine, because the differences blur within an hour of yard conversation. Photograph the odometer, the fuel or charge level and all four sides of the machine you finally choose, and ask the two questions the check cannot answer: what does the operator expect back at return, and who do you call if something changes mid-rental.</p>
      <p>The routine ends the conversation the same way it started: with a choice between machines that is now evidence-based. Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily from 09:00 to 21:00, can walk through the checks with you at handover, and a renter who selects by pre-ride check rides a machine whose every fault was either found or fixed before day one.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="faq">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>Can I test a scooter before choosing between models?</h2><p>Yes, and you should. Ask to sit on each candidate, hear them start cold, and roll them slowly to test the brakes. A five-minute walk-around on each machine tells you more than any specification list.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>What findings should rule out a rental machine?</h2><p>Safety findings, not cosmetic ones. Soft or grinding brakes, cracked tyres, a slipping transmission or dead brake lights end a candidacy. Scratches and scuffs are worth a photograph, not a rejection.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>How do the prices compare between the automatic classes?</h2><p>At this operator compact automatics like the Honda Click and Yamaha Mio rent at 150,000 VND per day and 700,000 VND per week. Larger machines like the Honda Vision and Air Blade rent at 200,000 VND per day and 1,000,000 VND per week. Confirm current figures at the counter.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>Should I choose the newest machine on the yard?</h2><p>Only if the checks agree. A newer fairing with a soft brake is a worse rental than an older machine with fresh tyres and firm progressive brakes. Maintenance beats mileage in this comparison.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>What equipment should come with the scooter?</h2><p>Ask for the exact list: helmet, lock, rain cover and any mount. Check the helmet fit and the lock condition as carefully as the machine itself, and confirm the keys before you leave.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>Who helps me run these checks at handover?</h2><p>The counter does. Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily from 09:00 to 21:00, can walk through the pre-ride checks with you and answer questions about what you find.</p></article>
    </section>

